### PART 1
By the fireside, Olaf glowed with cozy confidence, his warm, playful voice wrapped around the fragile Storythread like a mitten. Across from him, User waited with bright, mischievous curiosity, clutching a supply of Questions and clearly preferring surprises to sitting quietly through any ordinary tale.
Olaf’s story opened on a safe woodland path, where Little Red Riding Hood stood in her famous Hood with a Basket meant for Grandmother’s distant Cottage. The Forest waited nearby, hushed and tempting, while Olaf cheerfully prepared to make everything sound magical—and keep it from unraveling.
User (at Fireside): *leans forward, interrupting with sharp, eager urgency* "Wait! Before she goes in—do clouds ever get tired of floating, or are they just pretending?"
Olaf (at Fireside): *flustered, gently patting the Storythread back into place with a bright smile* "I think clouds get sleepy but keep floating because the sky gives excellent hugs! Now—whoosh—we’re back, and Little Red steps toward the Forest with her Basket!"
LittleRedRidingHood (at Path): *nervous but determined, steps beneath the first trees and tightens both hands around the Basket, glancing toward a glimmer just off the Path* "It is only a quick look... no, Grandmother first."
Just off the safe path, a silver flicker winked among fern fronds—perhaps dew, perhaps something else—while the deeper Forest swallowed ordinary birdsong.
BigBadWolf (at Forest): *steps from beside the silver flicker with a graceful bow, voice warm and smooth* "Good morning, dear child. What a charming Hood—and such a well-kept Basket. Are you bound somewhere important, all alone in this quiet wood?"
LittleRedRidingHood (at Forest): *politely guarded, tightens her grip on the Basket and glances back toward the Path* "Good morning, sir. I am expected at my Grandmother’s Cottage, and I know the way quite well."
BigBadWolf (at Forest): *smiles with polished warmth, tilting his head toward the dimmer trees* "How admirable, my dear. Grandmother must be very fortunate. Though cottages can be tricky in these woods—near the old birches, perhaps? Or beyond the brook? I only ask because there is a prettier way, quicker too, if one knows where to step."
LittleRedRidingHood (at Forest): *straightens her Hood, nervous yet proud, eyes flicking toward the dimmer trees* "Yes, Grandmother’s Cottage is beyond the brook, near the old birches. I am quite capable of finding it myself, sir—though... what sort of quicker way do you mean?"
BigBadWolf (at Forest): *smiles with practiced warmth, bowing aside toward a flower-strewn shadow path* "Capable? My dear, plainly so. That is why you will appreciate the clever little detour—violets, primroses, no thorns at all. It winds prettily before reaching those old birches beyond the brook. Do enjoy it; I shall take the duller way and trouble you no longer."
LittleRedRidingHood (at Forest): *hesitates, then lifts her chin and steps onto the shadow path, clutching the Basket* "I am choosing this because it seems useful, not because you suggested it, sir."
BigBadWolf (at Forest): *with a courteous bow, hides a satisfied gleam and slips toward the direct path through the trees* "As you wish, my dear. I would never dream of claiming credit for such an excellent choice. Enjoy the flowers."
Bent grasses marked the straighter route toward the brook, while the flowered detour curved away into slower, sweeter shadows.
User (at Fireside): *blurts out, leaning into the firelight with urgent curiosity* "Wait—do spoons know they’re spoons, or do they think they’re tiny shiny shovels?"
Olaf (at Fireside): *panicked, fumbles the Storythread, then catches it with a huge sunny grin* "I think spoons know they’re spoons—but dream of digging pudding! Whew! Back we hop: the Wolf has taken the direct path, zoom-zoom, toward poor Grandmother’s lonely Cottage before Little Red can get there!"
Beyond the brook, Grandmother’s Cottage sat beneath old birches, smoke curling gently from its chimney, its latch resting not quite secure.
Grandmother (at Cottage): *lonely but brisk, glances at the unsecured door latch, then turns away to straighten a teacup and smooth a blanket* "There now. No need to fuss over every little creak, is there?"
PART 1 ENDS
### PART 2
